  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nLow level stratus persists across parts of the area this afternoon. \nLocations that have broken out of the stratus have warmed to the 70s \nwhile those who remained under it are only seeing temperatures up to \nthe 60s. The broken up stratus will persist through the rest of the \nrest of the afternoon hours. The wave that will be responsible for \ntonight's heavy rain threat is currently located over Wyoming and \nwill encounter a very moist environment, characterized by \nprecipitable water values (PWAT) up to the max of climatology for \nthis time of year. Quasi-geostrophic (QG) forcing for ascent will \nincrease late this evening and night as this wave pushes into the \nright entrance region of an upper level jet. The low level jet will \nramp up with winds as strong as 40 knots, strengthening warm air \nadvection (WAA) in the low levels and tightening the thermal \ngradient. The tighter thermal gradient will result in a \nstrengthening elevated front and thus frontogenesis (FGEN). This \nfront will be in less stable environment characterized by moist \nneutral lapse rates. This will result in negative equivalent \npotential vorticity and thus the potential for a narrower, more \nintense band of rain. At the same time, weak CAPE values near to \nless than 500 J/kg will be advecting into the area from the south. \nThus, some weak thunderstorms are possible which could result in \nlocally heavier rainfall. The bulk of the rain looks to fall near \nand north of I-90 with the highest totals expected around highway-\n14. A cross section analysis shows deep omega (upward motion) over \nthe front, primarily over areas in the northwest part of the \nforecast area, generally northwest of Chamberlain, South Dakota to \nTracy, Minnesota line. This area looks to see the highest rainfall \ntonight due to the greatest forcing for ascent residing in this \narea. The rain will be on the move out Friday morning, especially \nduring the second half of the morning hours. While some lighter rain \nshowers may persist through the rest of the morning and into the \nearly afternoon timeframe, the bulk of the rain will be done \nfalling. In terms of rainfall amounts, a broad 1-2 inches is \nexpected along and north of I-90. Lower amounts down to 0.25 inches \nto 0.75 inches is expected south of the interstate. There remains \nthe potential for a localized maximum of 4+ inches of rain northwest \nof the previously mentioned line as supported by the HREF local \nprobability matched mean (LPMM), the individual hi-res members of \nthe HREF, and the RRFS and it's ensemble counter part the REFS. \n\nWhile the first half of Friday afternoon will be on the quieter \nside, some questions remain regarding a low end severe weather \npotential late Friday afternoon into the evening. Surface based \ninstability looks to potentially exceed 2,000 J/kg with sufficient \nvertical shear magnitudes of about 30 knots. An inverted trough may \nproduce enough low level convergence to potentially develop some \nstorms. The main limiting factor is that most of the better forcing \nfor ascent will not east/northeast of the forecast area. Think that \nif convection were to develop in this surface trough, it would be \ninitiated by the vort max itself which seems to be displaced a bit \nnorth of this boundary. As of now, think convection only has a 30-\n50% chance of developing due to the limited forcing for ascent. If \nconvection were to develop, it would develop primarily over \nnorthwest Iowa between 4 pm to 9 pm before racing southeast of the \narea. 0-7km theta-e difference of about 15-20K along with looping \nlow level hodographs suggest a low end damaging wind and an \nisolated tornado threat IF storms are able to develop. \n\nAn active pattern aloft will keep chances for rain in the forecast \nthrough the weekend and most of next week. Details remain uncertain \ndue to increasing variance amongst medium range guidance. \nTemperatures look to remain near to below average for this period of \ntime with highs in the 60s and 70s.",
